<span class="kicker">2026-08-31 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Redo a Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A paver walkway that’s settling, sprouting weeds, or just looking dated doesn’t always need to be torn out and started from scratch. In a lot of cases, the pavers themselves are still in good shape, and the actual problem is what’s underneath, a base that was never built deep enough, or one that’s shifted after years of freeze and thaw cycles. Figuring out which situation you’re actually dealing with is the first real decision in any redo project, since it determines whether you’re looking at a base repair using your existing pavers or a full replacement from the ground up.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we assess walkways that need redoing across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, on a regular basis, and the diagnosis stage matters just as much as the actual construction work that follows. This guide covers how to figure out what’s actually wrong with your walkway, how to decide between reusing existing pavers and full replacement, and how to keep costs reasonable throughout the process.</p>
<h2><b>Diagnosing Why Your Walkway Needs Redoing</b></h2>
<h3><b>Settling and Uneven Surfaces</b></h3>
<p>Dips, low spots, or pavers that rock slightly underfoot are almost always a base problem rather than a paver problem. This typically points to inadequate base depth or compaction from the original installation, or in some cases seasonal ground movement that’s gradually shifted things out of position over several years.</p>
<h3><b>Weeds Growing Through the Joints</b></h3>
<p>Persistent weed growth in the joints usually means the original joint sand, if it was polymeric sand at all, has broken down or washed out over time, leaving open space for weed seeds to establish. This is a more surface-level issue than settling and often has a more straightforward fix.</p>
<h3><b>Standing Water or Drainage Issues</b></h3>
<p>Water pooling on the walkway surface after rain points to a drainage slope problem, either the walkway was never built with proper pitch, or grading around it has changed over time, redirecting more water toward the walkway than it was originally designed to handle.</p>
<h3><b>Cracked or Damaged Individual Pavers</b></h3>
<p>A handful of cracked or chipped pavers scattered across an otherwise stable walkway is often a much smaller problem than widespread settling, frequently solvable by replacing just the damaged units rather than addressing anything structural underneath.</p>
<h3><b>Simply Outdated Style or Color</b></h3>
<p>Sometimes a walkway is structurally sound, no settling, no drainage issues, no significant paver damage, but the color, pattern, or paver style just doesn’t match updated landscaping or personal taste anymore. This is a purely aesthetic redo rather than a functional repair, which changes the calculus significantly.</p>
<h2><b>What Each Symptom Usually Means for Your Redo Approach</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Symptom</b></td><td><b>Likely Cause</b></td><td><b>Typical Fix</b></td></tr><tr><td>Settling or dips</td><td>Inadequate base depth or compaction</td><td>Lift pavers, rebuild base, reset pavers</td></tr><tr><td>Weeds in joints</td><td>Degraded or washed-out joint sand</td><td>Re-sand with polymeric sand</td></tr><tr><td>Standing water</td><td>Improper drainage slope</td><td>Regrade base, may require lifting pavers</td></tr><tr><td>A few cracked pavers</td><td>Localized damage, not base-related</td><td>Replace individual pavers</td></tr><tr><td>Outdated look only</td><td>No structural issue</td><td>Full replacement with new material, base often reusable</td></tr></tbody></table><h2><b>Should You Reuse Your Existing Pavers or Replace Them</b></h2>
<h3><b>When Reusing Makes Sense</b></h3>
<p>If your existing pavers are in good physical condition, no significant cracking, chipping, or wear, and your primary issue is settling, drainage, or joint sand degradation, reusing the existing pavers over a properly rebuilt base is often the more cost-effective path. This approach keeps material costs low while addressing the actual root cause of the problem.</p>
<h3><b>When Replacement Makes More Sense</b></h3>
<p>If the walkway’s problems stem primarily from wanting a different look, if the existing pavers show significant wear or damage across a large portion of the walkway, or if the paver product is no longer available and damaged pieces can’t be matched, full replacement often makes more sense than trying to preserve and reuse material that won’t give you the result you’re after anyway.</p>
<h3><b>A Middle Ground: Partial Replacement</b></h3>
<p>In some cases, particularly with localized damage, a partial replacement approach, keeping the majority of existing pavers while replacing only damaged sections, offers a reasonable middle ground. This works best when you can source matching replacement pavers, either from a saved surplus from the original installation or a close match from the manufacturer if the product is still available.</p>
<h2><b>Redoing a Walkway by Reusing Existing Pavers</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Carefully Lift the Existing Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Starting from one edge, lift pavers carefully and set them aside in the order and orientation they came out, which makes reinstallation more straightforward, particularly for patterned layouts like herringbone.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Remove and Assess the Old Base</b></h3>
<p>Once pavers are removed, assess what’s actually underneath. This tells you definitively whether the problem was inadequate base depth, poor compaction, or a drainage issue, information that’s harder to determine accurately while pavers are still in place.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Excavate Further if Needed</b></h3>
<p>If the existing base was too shallow, which is a common finding on older or poorly built walkways, excavate deeper to reach proper base depth, generally 4 to 6 inches depending on your soil type. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-thick-should-paver-base-be-for-a-walkway/"> how thick paver base should be</a> covers the depth standards worth targeting during this rebuild.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Build and Compact a Proper New Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers, compacting thoroughly with a plate compactor after each layer, correcting whatever base deficiency caused the original problem.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Address Drainage Slope</b></h3>
<p>If standing water was part of the original issue, use this rebuild as the opportunity to correct drainage slope, generally targeting 1 to 2 percent pitch directing water away from any nearby structures.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll fresh landscape fabric over the new base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand for a level surface.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Reinstall the Existing Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Lay your saved pavers back into position, following their original pattern and orientation, checking level frequently as you go.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Replace Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Even when reusing pavers, it’s worth replacing edge restraints entirely, since the original restraints have likely degraded or loosened over the years and are inexpensive relative to the rest of the project.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Fill Joints With Fresh Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep in new polymeric sand, even if the pavers themselves are being reused, since old, degraded joint sand is often part of what caused weed growth or joint instability in the first place.</p>

